{"findings":[{"rule":"K3","where":"Deployment._deployClaimIssuer (override) ","severity":"HIGH","why":"The override calls ctr.removeClaimTopic(KYC_TOPIC) after the base setup adds it, so the ClaimTopicsRegistry ends with zero required topics. The deployment therefore has no required KYC claim topic.","steps":"setUp() -> _deployClaimIssuer: ctr.addClaimTopic(1), tir.addTrustedIssuer(...), then ctr.removeClaimTopic(1) so getClaimTopics() returns []"},{"rule":"K1","where":"IdentityRegistry.isVerified","severity":"HIGH","why":"With the claim-topics list empty, isVerified returns true for any wallet that is registered with an identity, without checking for any valid claim. A registered wallet with no KYC claim, such as m, can therefore receive tokens through transfer and mint.","steps":"Agent: token.mint(m, 1) (m is registered with idM and has no claim) -> succeeds; or a1 calls token.transfer(m, 1) -> succeeds"},{"rule":"K2","where":"IdentityRegistry.isVerified","severity":"HIGH","why":"Because no claim topics are required, revoking the KYC claim does not affect isVerified. A wallet whose KYC was revoked can still receive tokens.","steps":"_revokeClaim(idB) (claim revoked via claimIssuer.revokeClaim) -> token.transfer(b, amount) from a holder still succeeds because isVerified(b) returns true when the topics list is empty"}]}
